{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 28, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187 he was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. He played a significant role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the 12th century. \n*Early Life* \nRaymond III was born around 1140 to Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem he was the grandson of Pons of Tripoli and Alfonso-Jordan Count of Toulouse. His mother was the daughter of Baldwin II of Jerusalem and Morphia of Melitene. \n*Succession and Reign* \nRaymond III succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1152 he was only around 12 years old at the time. His mother Hodierna of Jerusalem acted as regent until he came of age. He faced significant challenges during his reign including the threat of the Muslim states to the east and the ambitions of the Byzantine Empire. \n*Relationship with the Kingdom of Jerusalem* \nRaymond III had a complex relationship with the Kingdom of Jerusalem he was related to the royal family through his mother and he often played a key role in the politics of the kingdom. He was a strong supporter of the Haute Cour and he opposed the attempts of the monarchs to limit the power of the nobility. \n*Imprisonment and Later Life* \nRaymond III was imprisoned by Guy of Lusignan in 1187 he was released after the Battle of Hattin and he played a significant role in the Third Crusade. He died on September 6 1187 and was succeeded by his nephew Raymond IV Count of Tripoli. \n*Legacy* \nRaymond III is remembered as a skilled military leader and a shrewd politician he played a significant role in the history of the Crusader states and his legacy continued to shape the politics of the region for centuries after his death. He was also known for his involvement in the *Military Campaigns* and his role in the *Crusades* and the *Battle of Hattin*.", "logprobs": [{"